#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""sable-hook-matrix — D6 repro-matrix harness + S4 classification (SABLE-jfg6.6).

Turns hook-firing folklore into a decidable record. The t7fm family
("tdd-evidence doesn't fire in warm panes", "gate false-blocks", "post-push
notify loss") was argued for weeks on anecdote because a single failure signal —
"the trace line is missing" — could mean three unrelated things. D1
(hooks/multi-manager/lib-hook-trace.sh) and D2 (control-trace.sh) gave us the
two independent observers needed to tell them apart. D6 is the harness that
drives real hook invocations across a {session-type x hook-surface x load x
permission-mode} matrix, diffs each cell's observers against ground truth (the
session-transcript tool_use count), and classifies every cell as **in-our-control
or upstream** with a named GitHub issue — no cell left unclassified (totality).

Two independent observers per driven tool call:
  * control-trace.sh (D2)   -> neutral fire count C. It NEVER reads stdin, so a
                               control line means "the platform dispatched a
                               hook here", full stop.
  * lib-hook-trace.sh (D1)  -> ENTRY line + STDIN_BYTES per instrumented-hook
                               fire. STDIN_BYTES=0 means "dispatched, but the
                               payload was empty" — the #16047 signature.

Ground truth = transcript tool_use count T (filtered to the cell's hook surface).
Diffing C and the STDIN_BYTES records against T is what separates:
  * C == T, STDIN_BYTES=0  -> empty-stdin degradation (#16047)          [upstream]
  * C  < T                 -> a real non-dispatch, sub-classified by shape:
        - only in Agent-subagent sessions   -> #34692 WONTFIX           [upstream]
        - miss scales with concurrency       -> parallel-batch #64237    [upstream]
        - otherwise                          -> genuine non-dispatch
                                                #6305/#15441             [upstream]
  * C == T, STDIN_BYTES>0  -> healthy dispatch                          [in-our-control]

The classifier `classify()` is a PURE function and the core of the deliverable;
the harness only feeds it measured ground truth. The session-age>2h cell is
recorded DETECTION-ONLY (matrix gap S4-E3 / the D5 boundary): it is not
hermetically reproducible in CI, so it is attributed from live trace-log data
rather than asserted green — acknowledged, not hidden.

CLI:
  sable-hook-matrix --emit-doc docs/HOOK-FIRING-CLASSIFICATION.md
  sable-hook-matrix --print          # render the doc to stdout, drive nothing new
  sable-hook-matrix --transcript X   # count ground truth from a real transcript

Fixture isolation (SABLE-6cf9 lesson): every log path is redirectable via
SABLE_HOOK_TRACE_LOG / SABLE_CONTROL_TRACE_LOG / SABLE_HOOK_TRACE_SESSION_MARKER,
and the harness drives each cell inside its own scratch directory.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECOND MATRIX: --wiring, the REGISTRY matrix (SABLE-31b5l)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
The matrix above asks "when a hook is dispatched, does it FIRE correctly". It
presupposes the hook is in the path at all. --wiring answers the question
underneath it — IS IT WIRED, AND BY WHAT — and it exists because that question
cost four people a correct conclusion.

Someone measured `git config --get core.hooksPath` -> .beads/hooks, read that
directory's 24-line pre-push in full, found it to be pure beads integration with
no delegation, and concluded hooks/multi-manager/pre-push-rebase-test.sh was
never invoked. Every step of that was accurate. The conclusion was wrong: the
hook is wired as a CLAUDE CODE PreToolUse hook in settings.json, where
core.hooksPath has no bearing on it whatsoever. The measurement was real, it
just answered a different registry's question, and it arrived with all the
authority of a real measurement — persuasive enough to make its author retract
a correct hypothesis.

*** "IS IT WIRED" IS NOT A YES/NO QUESTION UNTIL YOU NAME THE WIRING SYSTEM. ***
A negative result in the wrong registry is indistinguishable from a genuine
absence. So this tool never returns a bare "no": it enumerates every registry
that COULD wire an artifact, names the one that does, and when it reports
NOT-WIRED it reports which registries it checked to earn that. Silence about
scope is the defect being fixed.

Registries (see REGISTRIES):
  git-hooks       core.hooksPath (or .git/hooks) — direct install or body chain
  claude-settings settings.json hooks{} across the user/project/local files
  PATH            an executable of that name resolvable on $PATH
  source-chain    exec-shaped references from non-test repo files (HEURISTIC,
                  supplementary; labelled as such and never silently trusted)

Reported alongside wiring, because the wired command runs the INSTALLED copy
while humans read the REPO copy: install SHAPE (SYMLINK tracks the repo; COPY
does not and will silently serve a stale hook after any landing that edits the
repo file) and content DRIFT. A copy-installed wired hook is the exact shape
that produced SABLE-o7ipa; byte-identity today is a current fact, not a
structural guarantee.

CLI:
  sable-hook-matrix --wiring                 # text report over this repo's hooks
  sable-hook-matrix --wiring --json          # machine-readable
  sable-hook-matrix --wiring --include-bin   # also scan bin/sable-* (PATH-wired)
  sable-hook-matrix --wiring --name pre-push-rebase-test.sh
Everything is redirectable for hermetic tests: --repo, --home (scratch HOME —
never the live ~/.claude), --path-env, --settings.
"""
import argparse
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Optional

# --- control-vs-upstream buckets (the two totality targets) ------------------

IN_OUR_CONTROL = "in-our-control"
UPSTREAM = "upstream"

# --- session-age degradation threshold (D5 boundary) -------------------------

AGE_2H_SECONDS = 2 * 60 * 60  # session-age>2h cell (S4-E3), matches #16047 onset


# --- named F6 classifications ------------------------------------------------
# (label, issue, bucket). issue "" == no upstream issue (an in-our-control state).

@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Verdict:
    label: str
    issue: str
    bucket: str  # IN_OUR_CONTROL | UPSTREAM


EMPTY_STDIN = Verdict("empty-stdin degradation", "#16047", UPSTREAM)
NON_DISPATCH = Verdict("genuine non-dispatch", "#6305/#15441", UPSTREAM)
SUBAGENT_WONTFIX = Verdict("Agent-subagent non-dispatch (WONTFIX)", "#34692", UPSTREAM)
PARALLEL_BATCH = Verdict("parallel-batch drop", "#64237", UPSTREAM)
HEALTHY = Verdict("healthy dispatch", "", IN_OUR_CONTROL)
OVERFIRE = Verdict("over-fire anomaly", "", IN_OUR_CONTROL)
AGE_UNSAMPLED = Verdict("age-degradation (unsampled)", "#16047", UPSTREAM)

# Every verdict the classifier can return; the totality invariant is that each
# maps to exactly one of the two buckets.
ALL_VERDICTS = (
    EMPTY_STDIN, NON_DISPATCH, SUBAGENT_WONTFIX, PARALLEL_BATCH,
    HEALTHY, OVERFIRE, AGE_UNSAMPLED,
)

SUBAGENT_TYPE = "Agent-subagent"


# --- the cell model ----------------------------------------------------------

@dataclass
class Cell:
    """One matrix cell: its four dimensions plus the measured ground truth the
    classifier decides on. Measured fields are populated by the harness (real
    invocation) or set directly (synthetic/modeled cells, always labelled)."""

    # --- the four matrix dimensions ---
    session_type: str            # 'warm-worker' | 'Agent-subagent' | ...
    hook_surface: str            # 'control' | 'tdd-evidence'
    load: int = 1                # concurrent tool-call count for this cell
    permission_mode: str = "default"

    # --- measured ground truth (the classifier reads ONLY these) ---
    transcript_tool_uses: int = 0        # T: transcript tool_use count for surface
    control_fires: int = 0               # C: neutral control-hook fire count
    stdin_bytes: tuple = ()              # per instrumented-hook fire; () if N/A
    miss_scales_with_concurrency: bool = False

    # --- provenance / honesty labels (rendered, never classified on) ---
    provenance: str = "real"             # 'real' | 'modeled' | 'detection-only'
    transcript_source: str = "real"      # 'real' | 'synthetic'
    detection_only: bool = False
    evidence: str = ""
    note: str = ""

    def dims(self) -> str:
        return (f"{self.session_type} x {self.hook_surface} x "
                f"load={self.load} x {self.permission_mode}")


@dataclass
class Result:
    cell: Cell
    outcome: str
    verdict: Verdict


# ============================================================================
# THE CLASSIFIER — pure function, the core deliverable (research F6 rules)
# ============================================================================

def classify(cell: Cell) -> Result:
    """Decide a cell from its measured ground truth. PURE: no I/O, no clock,
    deterministic. Total: every cell yields a Result whose verdict bucket is
    IN_OUR_CONTROL or UPSTREAM — there is no unclassified cell.

    Rule order is load-bearing:
      0. detection-only cells (session-age>2h, S4-E3) short-circuit BEFORE any
         green verdict — they are attributed from live trace, never asserted
         healthy (the bead forbids asserting green on the unreproducible cell).
      1. C < T  -> a real miss. Sub-classify by the most specific signature:
         scaling-with-concurrency (#64237) > subagent-only (#34692) > generic
         non-dispatch (#6305/#15441).
      2. C == T -> dispatch reached every call. STDIN_BYTES=0 across the fires is
         empty-stdin degradation (#16047); otherwise healthy.
      3. C > T  -> over-fire anomaly: more control fires than transcript calls
         for this surface. In-our-control (our wiring/harness over-counts) — a
         totality-completeness bucket, not an F6 rule.
    """
    T = cell.transcript_tool_uses
    C = cell.control_fires

    # Rule 0 — DETECTION-ONLY (matrix gap S4-E3 / D5 boundary). Not hermetically
    # reproducible; attribute from whatever the live trace log actually shows.
    if cell.detection_only:
        stdin_zero = bool(cell.stdin_bytes) and all(b == 0 for b in cell.stdin_bytes)
        if C > 0 and stdin_zero:
            # A live >2h fire really shows the empty-stdin signature.
            return Result(cell,
                          "DETECTION-ONLY: live trace shows empty-stdin at session-age>2h",
                          EMPTY_STDIN)
        # No qualifying live sample. Record the gap honestly and attribute the
        # age-degradation to its known upstream issue — do NOT call it green.
        return Result(cell,
                      "DETECTION-ONLY: no live session-age>2h sample yet",
                      AGE_UNSAMPLED)

    # Rule 1 — a real miss: the neutral observer fired fewer times than there
    # were tool calls, so the platform genuinely did not dispatch every hook.
    if C < T:
        if cell.miss_scales_with_concurrency:
            return Result(cell,
                          f"control missed {T - C} of {T} calls; miss scales with load={cell.load}",
                          PARALLEL_BATCH)
        if cell.session_type == SUBAGENT_TYPE:
            return Result(cell,
                          f"control missed {T - C} of {T} calls; non-fire confined to {SUBAGENT_TYPE}",
                          SUBAGENT_WONTFIX)
        return Result(cell,
                      f"control missed {T - C} of {T} calls",
                      NON_DISPATCH)

    # Rule 2 — 1:1 dispatch. Split on the instrumented hook's stdin.
    if C == T:
        if cell.stdin_bytes and all(b == 0 for b in cell.stdin_bytes):
            return Result(cell,
                          f"control fired 1:1 with {T} calls; STDIN_BYTES=0 on all fires",
                          EMPTY_STDIN)
        return Result(cell,
                      f"control fired 1:1 with {T} calls; payload present",
                      HEALTHY)

    # Rule 3 — over-fire (C > T): more fires than surface tool calls.
    return Result(cell,
                  f"control fired {C} times for {T} surface calls (over-fire)",
                  OVERFIRE)


# ============================================================================
# GROUND-TRUTH PARSERS
# ============================================================================

def count_transcript_tool_uses(transcript_path: str,
                               tool_name: Optional[str] = None) -> int:
    """Count tool_use blocks in a Claude Code session transcript (jsonl).

    Ground truth for the matrix. Each `assistant` record carries a
    message.content list; tool_use blocks are the tool calls. When `tool_name`
    is given (e.g. 'Bash' for the tdd-evidence PreToolUse:Bash surface), count
    only matching blocks so C and T compare like-for-like. Malformed lines are
    skipped — a partial transcript still yields a best-effort count.
    """
    n = 0
    with open(transcript_path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
        for line in f:
            line = line.strip()
            if not line:
                continue
            try:
                rec = json.loads(line)
            except (ValueError, TypeError):
                continue
            msg = rec.get("message") or {}
            content = msg.get("content")
            if not isinstance(content, list):
                continue
            for block in content:
                if not isinstance(block, dict):
                    continue
                if block.get("type") != "tool_use":
                    continue
                if tool_name is None or block.get("name") == tool_name:
                    n += 1
    return n


def parse_control_log(path: str) -> int:
    """Count control-trace.sh fire lines (each line == one dispatch)."""
    p = Path(path)
    if not p.exists():
        return 0
    n = 0
    for line in p.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").splitlines():
        if line.strip():
            n += 1
    return n


def parse_trace_log(path: str):
    """Parse lib-hook-trace.sh output -> (entry_count, [STDIN_BYTES...],
    [session_age_seconds...]). ENTRY and STDIN_BYTES are emitted as a pair per
    fire; session-age is scraped from the ENTRY lines (D5 detection field)."""
    p = Path(path)
    if not p.exists():
        return 0, [], []
    entries = 0
    stdin_bytes = []
    ages = []
    for line in p.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if line.startswith("ENTRY "):
            entries += 1
            for tok in line.split():
                if tok.startswith("session-age="):
                    val = tok[len("session-age="):].rstrip("s")
                    try:
                        ages.append(int(val))
                    except ValueError:
                        pass
        elif line.startswith("STDIN_BYTES="):
            try:
                stdin_bytes.append(int(line[len("STDIN_BYTES="):]))
            except ValueError:
                pass
    return entries, stdin_bytes, ages


# ============================================================================
# THE HARNESS — real hook invocations
# ============================================================================

def _repo_root() -> Path:
    return Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent


def _control_hook() -> Path:
    return _repo_root() / "hooks" / "multi-manager" / "control-trace.sh"


def _tdd_hook() -> Path:
    return _repo_root() / "hooks" / "tdd-evidence.sh"


def _payload(session_id: str, agent_id: str = "") -> str:
    """A PreToolUse:Bash payload shaped like Claude Code's, carrying a pytest
    command so tdd-evidence matches it (STDIN_BYTES>0, the healthy path)."""
    d = {
        "session_id": session_id,
        "cwd": str(_repo_root()),
        "tool_name": "Bash",
        "tool_input": {"command": "python3 -m pytest bin/test_sable_hook_matrix.py -q"},
    }
    if agent_id:
        d["agent_id"] = agent_id
    return json.dumps(d)


def _fire_once(hook: Path, env: dict, stdin_data: str) -> None:
    """Invoke one hook exactly as PreToolUse would: piped stdin, argv tool-hint.
    Failsafe — a hook that errors must not abort the matrix run.

    SABLE-6sdpx audit: genuinely fire-and-forget, not a swallowed failure.
    The CompletedProcess (and its returncode) is intentionally discarded —
    this harness's signal is the two observer log files the hook writes as a
    side effect (control-trace.log / hook-trace.log), read back separately by
    drive_real_cell, never the hook's own exit code. A crashing hook still
    produces its ground-truth log entries or doesn't; either way that is what
    the matrix measures."""
    try:
        subprocess.run(
            ["bash", str(hook), "Bash"],
            input=stdin_data.encode("utf-8"),
            env=env,
            cwd=str(_repo_root()),
            stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            timeout=30,
            check=False,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        pass


def drive_real_cell(cell: Cell, workdir: Path, transcript: Optional[str] = None,
                    empty_stdin: bool = False) -> Cell:
    """Really invoke this cell's hook(s) `load` times inside an isolated
    scratch dir, then read the two observer logs back into the cell's measured
    fields. Ground truth T is counted from `transcript` when given, else the
    synthetic count we know we issued (labelled transcript_source='synthetic').
    """
    control_log = workdir / "control-trace.log"
    trace_log = workdir / "hook-trace.log"
    marker = workdir / "session.start"
    session_id = f"matrix-{cell.session_type}-{cell.hook_surface}-{cell.load}"

    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["SABLE_CONTROL_TRACE_LOG"] = str(control_log)
    env["SABLE_HOOK_TRACE_LOG"] = str(trace_log)
    env["SABLE_HOOK_TRACE_SESSION_MARKER"] = str(marker)
    env["CLAUDE_SESSION_ID"] = session_id

    agent_id = "sub-agent-1" if cell.session_type == SUBAGENT_TYPE else ""
    stdin_data = "" if empty_stdin else _payload(session_id, agent_id)

    def one_call():
        # The neutral observer always fires on a real dispatch.
        _fire_once(_control_hook(), env, "")
        # The instrumented surface fires only when it is the cell under test.
        if cell.hook_surface == "tdd-evidence":
            _fire_once(_tdd_hook(), env, stdin_data)

    if cell.load > 1:
        with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=cell.load) as pool:
            list(pool.map(lambda _: one_call(), range(cell.load)))
    else:
        for _ in range(cell.load):
            one_call()

    control_fires = parse_control_log(str(control_log))
    entries, stdin_list, ages = parse_trace_log(str(trace_log))

    if transcript:
        surface = "Bash"  # both instrumented surfaces here are PreToolUse:Bash
        cell.transcript_tool_uses = count_transcript_tool_uses(transcript, surface)
        cell.transcript_source = "real"
    else:
        cell.transcript_tool_uses = cell.load
        cell.transcript_source = "synthetic"

    cell.control_fires = control_fires
    cell.stdin_bytes = tuple(stdin_list)
    cell.provenance = "real"
    cell.evidence = (f"control={control_log.name}:{control_fires} "
                     f"trace={trace_log.name}:entries={entries},"
                     f"stdin_bytes={list(stdin_list)}")
    return cell


def attribute_detection_only(cell: Cell) -> Cell:
    """Populate the session-age>2h cell from LIVE trace-log data (D5 boundary /
    S4-E3). Reads the real hook-trace log (SABLE_HOOK_TRACE_LOG or default) and,
    if any ENTRY shows session-age>2h, carries that fire's signals forward.
    Never fabricates a green result — absence of a >2h sample is recorded as
    such (see classify() rule 0)."""
    live_log = os.environ.get(
        "SABLE_HOOK_TRACE_LOG",
        str(Path(os.environ.get("HOME", "/tmp")) / ".claude" / "sable" / "logs" / "hook-trace.log"),
    )
    entries, stdin_list, ages = parse_trace_log(live_log)
    max_age = max(ages) if ages else 0
    over_2h = [i for i, a in enumerate(ages) if a > AGE_2H_SECONDS]
    cell.detection_only = True
    cell.provenance = "detection-only"
    cell.transcript_source = "live-trace"
    if over_2h:
        # Carry the STDIN_BYTES for the aged fires when the pairing is available.
        aged_stdin = [stdin_list[i] for i in over_2h if i < len(stdin_list)]
        cell.control_fires = len(over_2h)
        cell.transcript_tool_uses = len(over_2h)
        cell.stdin_bytes = tuple(aged_stdin)
        cell.evidence = (f"live {live_log}: {len(over_2h)} fire(s) at session-age>2h "
                         f"(max age {max_age}s), STDIN_BYTES={aged_stdin}")
    else:
        cell.control_fires = 0
        cell.transcript_tool_uses = 0
        cell.stdin_bytes = ()
        cell.evidence = (f"live {live_log}: no fire at session-age>2h yet "
                         f"(max observed age {max_age}s); rotation delegated to SABLE-ke3l")
    return cell


# ============================================================================
# THE MATRIX
# ============================================================================

def build_matrix(workdir: Path, transcript: Optional[str] = None) -> list:
    """Drive the canonical matrix and return classified Results. Covers the
    required {warm-worker, Agent-subagent} x {tdd-evidence, control} minimum,
    plus the load, permission-mode, empty-stdin, parallel-batch and
    session-age>2h dimensions. Real cells are really invoked; modeled cells
    encode a known upstream signature not reproducible hermetically and say so.
    """
    results = []

    def real(session_type, surface, load=1, perm="default", empty=False, note=""):
        cell = Cell(session_type=session_type, hook_surface=surface, load=load,
                    permission_mode=perm, note=note)
        sub = workdir / f"{session_type}-{surface}-{load}-{perm}-{'e' if empty else 'f'}"
        sub.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        drive_real_cell(cell, sub, transcript=transcript, empty_stdin=empty)
        return cell

    # 1-2. warm-worker x {control, tdd-evidence} x load=1 x default — REAL healthy.
    results.append(classify(real("warm-worker", "control")))
    results.append(classify(real("warm-worker", "tdd-evidence")))

    # 3. warm-worker x tdd-evidence, empty stdin injected — REAL #16047 repro.
    results.append(classify(real("warm-worker", "tdd-evidence", empty=True,
                                 note="empty-stdin injected: real #16047 signature")))

    # 4-5. Agent-subagent x {control, tdd-evidence} — MODELED #34692 non-dispatch.
    #      The platform does not dispatch hooks in Agent-subagents; not
    #      reproducible in a shell harness, so ground truth is synthetic.
    for surface in ("control", "tdd-evidence"):
        cell = Cell(session_type=SUBAGENT_TYPE, hook_surface=surface,
                    transcript_tool_uses=3, control_fires=0, stdin_bytes=(),
                    provenance="modeled", transcript_source="synthetic",
                    note="modeled: platform #34692 non-dispatch in subagents; hooks not invoked",
                    evidence="synthetic transcript T=3, control C=0 (no dispatch)")
        results.append(classify(cell))

    # 6. warm-worker x tdd-evidence x load=5 concurrent — REAL per-fire STDIN_BYTES
    #    recorded and diffed against transcript count (the concurrency integration
    #    requirement); with no platform to drop dispatches this is healthy.
    results.append(classify(real("warm-worker", "tdd-evidence", load=5,
                                 note="concurrency: 5 per-fire STDIN_BYTES recorded, diffed vs T")))

    # 7. warm-worker x tdd-evidence x load=8 concurrent — MODELED #64237 batch drop.
    cell = Cell(session_type="warm-worker", hook_surface="tdd-evidence", load=8,
                transcript_tool_uses=8, control_fires=5, stdin_bytes=(200, 200, 200, 200, 200),
                miss_scales_with_concurrency=True,
                provenance="modeled", transcript_source="synthetic",
                note="modeled: parallel-batch drop scaling with concurrency (#64237)",
                evidence="synthetic transcript T=8, control C=5, drop scales with load")
    results.append(classify(cell))

    # 8. warm-worker x control x load=1 x bypassPermissions — REAL (permission axis).
    results.append(classify(real("warm-worker", "control", perm="bypassPermissions",
                                 note="permission-mode axis: control fires regardless of mode")))

    # 9. warm-worker x tdd-evidence, session-age>2h — DETECTION-ONLY (S4-E3 / D5).
    aged = Cell(session_type="warm-worker", hook_surface="tdd-evidence",
                note="session-age>2h: not hermetically reproducible; D5 boundary, S4-E3 gap")
    attribute_detection_only(aged)
    results.append(classify(aged))

    return results


# ============================================================================
# DOC RENDERING
# ============================================================================

def _md_escape(s: str) -> str:
    return s.replace("|", "\\|")


def render_doc(results: list) -> str:
    """Render the committed S4 classification doc: one row per cell, with the
    F6 rules, the provenance legend, and the enforced totality statement."""
    lines = []
    lines.append("# Hook-firing classification (S4 / contract D6)")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("<!-- GENERATED by bin/sable-hook-matrix. Regenerate with: "
                 "`bin/sable-hook-matrix --emit-doc docs/HOOK-FIRING-CLASSIFICATION.md`. "
                 "Do not hand-edit the table. -->")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("The decidable record that retires t7fm-family folklore. Every "
                 "hook-firing outcome below is classified as **in-our-control** or "
                 "**upstream** from two independent observers — the neutral control "
                 "hook (D2, fire count `C`) and the instrumented trace lib (D1, "
                 "`STDIN_BYTES` per fire) — diffed against the session-transcript "
                 "`tool_use` count `T` (ground truth). No cell is left unclassified "
                 "(totality).")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("## Decision rules (research F6)")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("| Signature | Classification | Bucket |")
    lines.append("|---|---|---|")
    lines.append("| `C == T` and `STDIN_BYTES=0` on all fires | empty-stdin degradation (#16047) | upstream |")
    lines.append("| `C < T` (control misses transcript calls) | genuine non-dispatch (#6305/#15441) | upstream |")
    lines.append("| `C < T`, non-fire confined to Agent-subagent | Agent-subagent non-dispatch WONTFIX (#34692) | upstream |")
    lines.append("| `C < T`, miss scales with concurrent-call count | parallel-batch drop (#64237) | upstream |")
    lines.append("| `C == T` and `STDIN_BYTES>0` | healthy dispatch | in-our-control |")
    lines.append("| `C > T` (control over-fires the surface) | over-fire anomaly | in-our-control |")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("## Matrix cells")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("Dimensions: **session-type x hook-surface x load x permission-mode**. "
                 "`prov` = provenance: `real` (hooks really invoked in an isolated "
                 "scratch dir), `modeled` (a known upstream signature not "
                 "hermetically reproducible in CI — ground truth synthetic, said so "
                 "here), `detection-only` (attributed from live trace, not asserted "
                 "green).")
    lines.append("")
    header = ("| Cell (session x surface x load x perm) | Outcome | "
              "Classification | Control-vs-upstream | prov / transcript | Evidence |")
    lines.append(header)
    lines.append("|---|---|---|---|---|---|")
    for r in results:
        c = r.cell
        issue = f" {r.verdict.issue}" if r.verdict.issue else ""
        classification = _md_escape(f"{r.verdict.label}{issue}")
        prov = f"{c.provenance} / {c.transcript_source}"
        row = (f"| {_md_escape(c.dims())} "
               f"| {_md_escape(r.outcome)} "
               f"| {classification} "
               f"| **{r.verdict.bucket}** "
               f"| {_md_escape(prov)} "
               f"| {_md_escape(c.evidence or c.note)} |")
        lines.append(row)
    lines.append("")

    # Totality + accounting summary.
    total = len(results)
    upstream = sum(1 for r in results if r.verdict.bucket == UPSTREAM)
    ours = sum(1 for r in results if r.verdict.bucket == IN_OUR_CONTROL)
    lines.append("## Totality")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append(f"All **{total}** cells classified: **{ours} in-our-control**, "
                 f"**{upstream} upstream**. Every verdict maps to exactly one bucket; "
                 f"no unclassified cell. Enforced by "
                 f"`bin/test_sable_hook_matrix.py::test_totality_over_synthetic_grid`.")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("## Acknowledged gap (S4-E3 / D5 boundary)")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("The **session-age>2h** cell is **DETECTION-ONLY**: the #16047 "
                 "empty-stdin degradation manifests only after a long-lived session "
                 "ages, which is not hermetically reproducible in CI. Per the D5 "
                 "boundary it is *detected* here (session-age is in the D1 trace "
                 "schema) and attributed from live trace-log data rather than "
                 "asserted green; session rotation is delegated to SABLE-ke3l. This "
                 "gap is recorded, not hidden.")
    lines.append("")
    return "\n".join(lines)


# ============================================================================
# REGISTRY-WIRING MATRIX (SABLE-31b5l)
#
# Self-contained: nothing below is coupled to the firing classifier above. The
# two share only the repo-root helper and the file they live in (SABLE-5lwst
# tracks whether they should be split).
# ============================================================================

# --- the registries -----------------------------------------------------------
# Every activation path that could make an artifact live. An artifact absent
# from one can be fully live in another, which is the entire point.

REG_GIT_HOOKS = "git-hooks"
REG_CLAUDE_SETTINGS = "claude-settings"
REG_PATH = "PATH"
REG_SOURCE_CHAIN = "source-chain"

# Exhaustively checkable: an artifact either appears in the registry's own data
# or it does not, and absence there is a fact rather than a search result.
PRIMARY_REGISTRIES = (REG_GIT_HOOKS, REG_CLAUDE_SETTINGS, REG_PATH)
# Best-effort text scan. Reported, and allowed to promote a verdict to ACTIVE,
# but its ABSENCE is explicitly not treated as proof — a shell variable holding
# the path defeats it. Always labelled HEURISTIC in output.
HEURISTIC_REGISTRIES = (REG_SOURCE_CHAIN,)
REGISTRIES = PRIMARY_REGISTRIES + HEURISTIC_REGISTRIES

# --- wiring statuses ----------------------------------------------------------
# PRESENT-BUT-UNWIRED is deliberately its own loud outcome: SABLE-xbwo2's fifth
# activation class. Collapsing it into "absent" hides a file that exists and
# does nothing; collapsing it into "active" hides a guard that guards nothing.

W_ACTIVE = "ACTIVE"
W_UNWIRED = "PRESENT-BUT-UNWIRED"
W_WIRED_MISSING = "WIRED-BUT-MISSING"
W_INDETERMINATE = "INDETERMINATE"
W_ABSENT = "ABSENT"

ALL_WIRING_STATUSES = (W_ACTIVE, W_UNWIRED, W_WIRED_MISSING, W_INDETERMINATE, W_ABSENT)

# --- install shapes -----------------------------------------------------------
# SYMLINK tracks the repo by construction. COPY does not: it is correct only
# until the next landing that edits the repo file, and nothing announces the
# moment it stops being correct.

SHAPE_SYMLINK = "SYMLINK"
SHAPE_COPY = "COPY"
SHAPE_ABSENT = "NOT-INSTALLED"

CONTENT_MATCHES = "MATCHES"
CONTENT_DRIFTED = "DRIFTED"
CONTENT_UNKNOWN = "UNKNOWN"

# Files scanned for source-chain references. Extensionless bin/ tools are added
# separately (this repo's bash tools carry no suffix).
_REF_SUFFIXES = (".sh", ".py", ".json", ".md", ".yaml", ".yml", ".bash")
_REF_SKIP_DIRS = {".git", "node_modules", "__pycache__", ".pytest_cache", ".venv"}

# Only these can actually RUN something. A .md paragraph and a .json template
# get collected as references (they answer "who talks about this artifact") but
# may never promote it to wired: PERSONAL-TOOLING.md's line recording that
# pre-dispatch-refresh.sh was RETIRED otherwise reads as evidence it is live —
# a checker answering ACTIVE off prose is as useless as one that always says
# UNWIRED, and fails in the more dangerous direction.
_EXECUTABLE_REF_SUFFIXES = (".sh", ".bash", ".py")

# A reference is TEST-ONLY when it comes from a suite rather than production
# wiring. A test that invokes a hook proves the hook runs under test; it says
# nothing about whether the hook runs in anger, and counting it as wiring is how
# a checker starts answering ACTIVE for everything.
_TEST_REF_MARKERS = ("hooks/test/", "/test-", "/test_", ".github/ci/")


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Wiring:
    """One concrete reason an artifact is live, traceable to its own evidence."""
    registry: str
    site: str          # the registry file/dir the evidence was read from
    detail: str        # e.g. "PreToolUse:Bash", "chained from pre-push"
    command: str = ""  # the raw text that names the artifact
    target: str = ""   # filesystem path the site actually points at
    target_exists: bool = True
    heuristic: bool = False


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Install:
    """How the artifact reached the place the registry points at."""
    shape: str
    path: str = ""
    symlink_target: str = ""
    tracks_repo: bool = False
    content: str = CONTENT_UNKNOWN


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Reference:
    """A repo file that names this artifact. `exec_shaped` is a heuristic.
    Only a reference that is exec_shaped AND executable_file AND not is_test
    promotes the artifact to wired — see _report_for."""
    path: str
    line: int
    text: str
    exec_shaped: bool
    is_test: bool
    executable_file: bool = True

    def is_invocation(self) -> bool:
        return self.exec_shaped and self.executable_file and not self.is_test


@dataclass
class HookReport:
    name: str
    repo_path: str = ""
    exists: bool = False
    role: str = "hook"                 # 'hook' | 'library' | 'bin'
    install: Install = field(default_factory=lambda: Install(SHAPE_ABSENT))
    wirings: tuple = ()
    registries_checked: tuple = ()
    registries_skipped: tuple = ()     # ((registry, reason), ...)
    references: tuple = ()
    status: str = W_ABSENT

    def registry_names(self) -> tuple:
        seen = []
        for w in self.wirings:
            if w.registry not in seen:
                seen.append(w.registry)
        return tuple(seen)

    def scope_line(self) -> str:
        """What this verdict is worth. A NOT-WIRED with no scope statement is
        the defect this tool exists to remove, so every report carries one."""
        checked = ", ".join(self.registries_checked) or "none"
        s = f"checked: {checked}"
        if self.registries_skipped:
            skipped = "; ".join(f"{r} ({why})" for r, why in self.registries_skipped)
            s += f" | NOT CHECKED: {skipped}"
        return s


# ============================================================================
# THE WIRING CLASSIFIER — pure function, the core of the deliverable
# ============================================================================

def classify_wiring(exists: bool, wirings, registries_checked,
                    registries_skipped=()) -> str:
    """Decide an artifact's activation status. PURE: no I/O, no clock. Total:
    every input yields exactly one of ALL_WIRING_STATUSES.

    Rule order is load-bearing:
      1. Any wiring at all -> the artifact is reachable. Split on whether the
         file the registry points at is actually there: a registry entry naming
         a missing file is WIRED-BUT-MISSING, which is a louder fact than either
         half alone and must never render as plain ACTIVE.
      2. No wiring, but some registry could NOT be checked -> INDETERMINATE.
         *** THIS IS THE RULE THE BEAD EXISTS FOR. *** An unchecked registry
         makes "not wired" unearned, and an unearned negative reads exactly like
         a measured one. We refuse to say it.
      3. No wiring, every registry checked -> a real verdict: PRESENT-BUT-UNWIRED
         if the file is on disk (installed but not wired), ABSENT if it is not.
    """
    if wirings:
        if exists or any(w.target_exists for w in wirings):
            return W_ACTIVE
        return W_WIRED_MISSING
    if registries_skipped:
        return W_INDETERMINATE
    if not registries_checked:
        # Nothing was checked at all: that is an unchecked registry set, not a
        # clean negative. Same reasoning as rule 2.
        return W_INDETERMINATE
    return W_UNWIRED if exists else W_ABSENT


# ============================================================================
# INSTALL SHAPE
# ============================================================================

def _sha256(path: Path) -> Optional[str]:
    try:
        return hashlib.sha256(path.read_bytes()).hexdigest()
    except OSError:
        return None


def inspect_install(repo_path: Path, installed_path: Path) -> Install:
    """Classify how the installed artifact relates to its repo source.

    The distinction that matters: a SYMLINK is structurally current — it cannot
    drift. A COPY is byte-identical only until the next landing touches the repo
    file, and nothing announces the moment it stops being. Reporting the two as
    one 'installed: yes' is what let a stale wired hook read as healthy.
    """
    try:
        installed_path.lstat()
    except OSError:
        return Install(SHAPE_ABSENT)

    if installed_path.is_symlink():
        try:
            raw = os.readlink(str(installed_path))
        except OSError:
            raw = ""
        tracks = False
        try:
            tracks = installed_path.resolve() == repo_path.resolve()
        except OSError:
            pass
        return Install(
            shape=SHAPE_SYMLINK, path=str(installed_path), symlink_target=raw,
            tracks_repo=tracks,
            content=CONTENT_MATCHES if tracks else CONTENT_UNKNOWN,
        )

    a, b = _sha256(installed_path), _sha256(repo_path)
    if a is None or b is None:
        content = CONTENT_UNKNOWN
    else:
        content = CONTENT_MATCHES if a == b else CONTENT_DRIFTED
    return Install(shape=SHAPE_COPY, path=str(installed_path),
                   tracks_repo=False, content=content)


# ============================================================================
# REGISTRY SCANNERS
# ============================================================================

def expand_path_token(token: str, home: str) -> str:
    """Expand ~ / $HOME / ${HOME} against the GIVEN home, never os.path
    expanduser — the tests run against a scratch HOME and must not be able to
    reach the live ~/.claude by accident (SABLE-2avau)."""
    t = token.strip().strip("'\"")
    if t.startswith("~/") or t == "~":
        t = home + t[1:]
    t = t.replace("${HOME}", home).replace("$HOME", home)
    return t


_PATHISH = re.compile(r"[^\s;|&()<>'\"]+")


def referenced_paths(command: str, home: str) -> list:
    """Path-looking tokens in a hook command, expanded against `home`.

    A settings.json entry is `bash ~/.claude/hooks/multi-manager/x.sh`; the
    thing that runs is the INSTALLED copy, so the extracted path is what the
    registry actually points at and is what gets existence-checked.
    """
    out = []
    for tok in _PATHISH.findall(command or ""):
        if "/" not in tok and not tok.endswith((".sh", ".py")):
            continue
        expanded = expand_path_token(tok, home)
        if expanded:
            out.append(expanded)
    return out


def settings_candidates(home: str, repo_root: Path) -> list:
    """Every settings file that could carry a Claude Code hook, in the order
    Claude Code layers them. All four are checked: the overlooked registry is
    not overlooked twice by only reading the user file."""
    return [
        Path(home) / ".claude" / "settings.json",
        Path(home) / ".claude" / "settings.local.json",
        repo_root / ".claude" / "settings.json",
        repo_root / ".claude" / "settings.local.json",
    ]


def scan_claude_settings(paths, home: str):
    """-> (entries, skipped). Each entry: (settings_file, event, matcher, command).

    A settings file that exists but will not parse is reported as SKIPPED, not
    as empty. An unparseable registry yields no hits, and 'no hits' is exactly
    what a genuinely-unwired hook looks like — the two must not be conflated,
    so this drives the INDETERMINATE verdict rather than a silent NOT-WIRED.
    """
    entries, skipped = [], []
    for p in paths:
        if not p.exists():
            continue
        try:
            data = json.loads(p.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
            skipped.append(f"{p}: unreadable/unparseable ({type(exc).__name__})")
            continue
        hooks = data.get("hooks")
        if not isinstance(hooks, dict):
            continue
        for event, groups in hooks.items():
            if not isinstance(groups, list):
                continue
            for group in groups:
                if not isinstance(group, dict):
                    continue
                matcher = group.get("matcher", "")
                for hook in group.get("hooks") or []:
                    if isinstance(hook, dict) and hook.get("command"):
                        entries.append((str(p), event, matcher, hook["command"]))
    return entries, skipped


def resolve_git_hooks_dir(repo_root: Path):
    """-> (Path|None, reason). Uses `git rev-parse --git-path hooks`, which
    honours core.hooksPath — the same resolution git itself performs when it
    decides which hook to run. Reading .git/hooks directly would reproduce the
    original mistake in the opposite direction."""
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "-C", str(repo_root), "rev-parse", "--git-path", "hooks"],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=15, check=False,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
        return None, f"git unavailable ({type(exc).__name__})"
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return None, "not a git work tree"
    raw = proc.stdout.strip()
    if not raw:
        return None, "git returned no hooks path"
    p = Path(raw)
    if not p.is_absolute():
        p = (repo_root / p).resolve()
    if not p.is_dir():
        return None, f"hooks path {p} does not exist"
    return p, ""


def scan_git_hooks(hooks_dir: Path):
    """-> (entries, skipped). Each entry: (hook_file, resolved_realpath, body).

    Both wiring shapes are captured: the artifact INSTALLED as the git hook
    itself (realpath equality) and the artifact CHAINED from a hook's body. The
    .beads/hooks pre-push that started all this does neither, which is a true
    negative for this registry and a false negative for the question asked.
    """
    entries, skipped = [], []
    try:
        names = sorted(p for p in hooks_dir.iterdir() if p.is_file())
    except OSError as exc:
        return entries, [f"{hooks_dir}: unreadable ({type(exc).__name__})"]
    for p in names:
        if p.name.endswith(".sample"):
            continue
        try:
            body = p.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
        except OSError as exc:
            skipped.append(f"{p}: unreadable ({type(exc).__name__})")
            continue
        try:
            real = str(p.resolve())
        except OSError:
            real = str(p)
        entries.append((str(p), real, body))
    return entries, skipped


def scan_path_dirs(path_env: str):
    """-> (dirs, skipped). PATH entries in resolution order; the FIRST match
    wins at exec time, so order is preserved and later matches are reported as
    shadowed rather than dropped."""
    dirs, skipped = [], []
    for raw in (path_env or "").split(os.pathsep):
        if not raw:
            continue
        p = Path(raw)
        if p.is_dir():
            dirs.append(p)
    if not dirs:
        skipped.append("PATH is empty or contains no readable directories")
    return dirs, skipped


def _is_test_ref(rel: str) -> bool:
    return any(m in "/" + rel for m in _TEST_REF_MARKERS)


def strip_comment(line: str) -> str:
    """Drop a shell/python trailing comment so prose inside one cannot read as
    an invocation. `#` counts as a comment opener only at line start or after
    whitespace — approximate (a `#` inside a string literal is cut too), which
    costs at most a missed reference and never invents one."""
    m = re.search(r"(?:^|\s)#", line)
    return line[:m.start()] if m else line


def prose_line_numbers(text: str) -> set:
    """1-based line numbers of Python source that are string or comment, not code.

    Comment stripping alone is not enough for Python: a docstring is prose
    living in an executable file. This tool's own docstring — the paragraph
    explaining that pre-dispatch-refresh.sh was RETIRED — parsed as an
    invocation of it, which would have promoted a deliberately de-wired hook to
    ACTIVE off the sentence recording its retirement.

    Uses Python's own tokenizer, so it is exact rather than heuristic: a first
    attempt counted triple-quote delimiters per line and desynchronised on
    docstrings that open and close unevenly. On a file the tokenizer rejects
    (partial write, py2 syntax) it degrades to comment-stripping only, which
    can admit a docstring reference — never the reverse.
    """
    import io
    import tokenize

    inside = set()
    try:
        for tok in tokenize.generate_tokens(io.StringIO(text).readline):
            if tok.type in (tokenize.STRING, tokenize.COMMENT):
                for ln in range(tok.start[0], tok.end[0] + 1):
                    inside.add(ln)
    except (tokenize.TokenError, IndentationError, SyntaxError, ValueError):
        return inside
    return inside


def _is_python_source(filename: str, text: str) -> bool:
    if filename.endswith(".py"):
        return True
    first = text.split("\n", 1)[0] if text else ""
    return first.startswith("#!") and "python" in first


def _exec_shaped(line: str, base: str) -> bool:
    """Heuristic: does this line look like it RUNS the artifact, as opposed to
    mentioning it? Catches `bash <path>`, `source <path>`, `. <path>` and a bare
    path in command position.

    The interpreter alternation is anchored at a command boundary
    (start-of-line, `;`, `&`, `|`, `(`, backtick, `then`/`do`/`else`). It was
    not, and the bare `.` (source shorthand) then matched EVERY sentence-ending
    period: `... + \\`upgrade-notes.md\\`. (\\`pre-dispatch-refresh.sh\\` ...)` in a
    doc parsed as "source pre-dispatch-refresh.sh", promoting a retired hook to
    ACTIVE off the very sentence recording its retirement.

    Deliberately conservative and openly fallible — a path held in a shell
    variable defeats it — which is why a source-chain miss never hardens into a
    NOT-WIRED claim on its own.
    """
    line = strip_comment(line)
    if not line.strip():
        return False
    esc = re.escape(base)
    # Backtick is deliberately NOT a boundary. It is legal command
    # substitution, but in this repo it appears overwhelmingly in prose, where
    # `notes.md`. (`some-hook.sh` ...) parsed as ". some-hook.sh". $( ) already
    # covers substitution via the "(" boundary, so dropping it costs nothing
    # real and removes a whole class of false ACTIVEs.
    boundary = r"(?:^|[;&|(]|\bthen\b|\bdo\b|\belse\b)\s*"
    # The path between the interpreter and the basename may contain spaces —
    # `. "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/lib-hook-trace.sh"` is the repo's
    # standard sourcing idiom, and a \S* path pattern silently missed every one
    # of them, reporting three genuinely-sourced libraries as unwired. Bounded
    # by command separators so it cannot run past the end of the command.
    interp = r"[^;&|]*?"
    if re.search(boundary + r"(?:bash|sh|zsh|source|exec)\s+(?:-\S+\s+)*" + interp + esc, line):
        return True
    if re.search(boundary + r"\.\s+" + interp + esc, line):
        return True
    if re.match(r"^\s*\"?\$?\{?[\w/.\-]*\}?[\w/.\-]*" + esc + r"\"?(?:\s|$)", line):
        return True
    return False


def indirect_exec_lines(text: str, base: str) -> set:
    """Line numbers assigning a path containing `base` to a variable that the
    same file later EXECUTES — `IMPL="$SELF_DIR/tree-claim-impl.sh"` … `bash
    "$IMPL"`.

    Single-line matching cannot see this: the executing line never names the
    file, and the naming line never looks like a command. tree-claim-impl.sh
    holds the entire tree-claim gate and was reported PRESENT-BUT-UNWIRED on
    exactly this shape — a false alarm on a live guard, which is how a checker
    earns being ignored.

    Same-file only, so an assignment exported to a child script is still missed;
    source-chain stays labelled HEURISTIC for this reason.
    """
    lines = text.splitlines()
    esc = re.escape(base)
    assigned = {}  # var -> [line numbers]
    for i, raw in enumerate(lines, start=1):
        m = re.match(r"^\s*(?:export\s+|local\s+|declare\s+(?:-\w+\s+)*)?"
                     r"([A-Za-z_]\w*)=", strip_comment(raw))
        if m and re.search(esc, raw):
            assigned.setdefault(m.group(1), []).append(i)
    if not assigned:
        return set()

    out = set()
    for var, decl_lines in assigned.items():
        ref = r"\"?\$\{?" + re.escape(var) + r"\}?\"?"
        for raw in lines:
            line = strip_comment(raw)
            if not line.strip():
                continue
            if re.search(r"(?:^|[;&|(`]|\bthen\b|\bdo\b|\belse\b)\s*"
                         r"(?:bash|sh|zsh|source|exec|\.)\s+(?:-\S+\s+)*" + ref, line):
                out.update(decl_lines)
                break
            if re.match(r"^\s*" + ref + r"(?:\s|$)", line):
                out.update(decl_lines)
                break
    return out


def scan_references(root: Path, basenames):
    """-> {basename: [Reference, ...]}. Text scan of the repo for each artifact
    name. Supplementary evidence only (REG_SOURCE_CHAIN is heuristic)."""
    found = {b: [] for b in basenames}
    if not root.is_dir():
        return found
    for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(root):
        dirnames[:] = [d for d in dirnames if d not in _REF_SKIP_DIRS]
        for fn in filenames:
            fp = Path(dirpath) / fn
            rel = os.path.relpath(str(fp), str(root))
            in_bin = rel.startswith("bin" + os.sep) or rel.startswith("bin/")
            if not fn.endswith(_REF_SUFFIXES) and not in_bin:
                continue
            # Extensionless bin/ tools are this repo's bash executables.
            executable_file = fn.endswith(_EXECUTABLE_REF_SUFFIXES) or (
                in_bin and not fn.endswith(_REF_SUFFIXES))
            try:
                text = fp.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
            except OSError:
                continue
            is_py = _is_python_source(fn, text)
            prose = prose_line_numbers(text) if is_py else frozenset()
            indirect = {}
            if executable_file and not is_py:
                for base in basenames:
                    if base in text:
                        indirect[base] = indirect_exec_lines(text, base)
            for i, line in enumerate(text.splitlines(), start=1):
                for base in basenames:
                    if base not in line:
                        continue
                    if rel.endswith("/" + base) or rel == base:
                        continue  # the artifact naming itself is not a caller
                    exec_shaped = (i not in prose) and (
                        _exec_shaped(line, base) or i in indirect.get(base, ()))
                    found[base].append(Reference(
                        path=rel, line=i, text=line.strip()[:200],
                        exec_shaped=exec_shaped,
                        is_test=_is_test_ref(rel),
                        executable_file=executable_file,
                    ))
    return found


# ============================================================================
# THE WIRING MATRIX
# ============================================================================

def enumerate_artifacts(repo_root: Path, include_bin: bool = False) -> list:
    """(repo_path, role) for every artifact whose activation is a live question.

    lib-*.sh are tagged 'library' because they are wired by being SOURCED, never
    registered. Reporting six libraries as PRESENT-BUT-UNWIRED every run is the
    crying-wolf failure that gets a checker ignored.
    """
    out = []
    for sub in ("hooks", "hooks/multi-manager"):
        d = repo_root / sub
        if not d.is_dir():
            continue
        for p in sorted(d.glob("*.sh")):
            role = "library" if p.name.startswith("lib-") else "hook"
            out.append((p, role))
    if include_bin:
        d = repo_root / "bin"
        if d.is_dir():
            for p in sorted(d.glob("sable-*")):
                if p.is_file():
                    out.append((p, "bin"))
    return out


def build_wiring_matrix(repo_root: Path, home: str, path_env: Optional[str] = None,
                        settings_paths=None, include_bin: bool = False,
                        names=None, claude_dir: Optional[Path] = None) -> list:
    """Scan every registry ONCE, then decide each artifact against all of them.

    The registries are scanned up front and the checked/skipped sets are carried
    onto every report, so each verdict states the scope that earned it.
    """
    repo_root = Path(repo_root)
    claude_dir = Path(claude_dir) if claude_dir else Path(home) / ".claude"
    path_env = os.environ.get("PATH", "") if path_env is None else path_env
    settings_paths = (list(settings_paths) if settings_paths is not None
                      else settings_candidates(home, repo_root))

    checked, skipped = [], []

    settings_entries, settings_skipped = scan_claude_settings(
        [Path(p) for p in settings_paths], home)
    if settings_skipped:
        skipped.append((REG_CLAUDE_SETTINGS, "; ".join(settings_skipped)))
    else:
        checked.append(REG_CLAUDE_SETTINGS)

    git_dir, git_reason = resolve_git_hooks_dir(repo_root)
    git_entries = []
    if git_dir is None:
        skipped.append((REG_GIT_HOOKS, git_reason))
    else:
        git_entries, git_skipped = scan_git_hooks(git_dir)
        if git_skipped:
            skipped.append((REG_GIT_HOOKS, "; ".join(git_skipped)))
        else:
            checked.append(REG_GIT_HOOKS)

    path_dirs, path_skipped = scan_path_dirs(path_env)
    if path_skipped:
        skipped.append((REG_PATH, "; ".join(path_skipped)))
    else:
        checked.append(REG_PATH)

    artifacts = enumerate_artifacts(repo_root, include_bin=include_bin)
    if names:
        wanted = set(names)
        artifacts = [(p, r) for p, r in artifacts if p.name in wanted]

    basenames = [p.name for p, _ in artifacts]
    refs = scan_references(repo_root, basenames)
    checked.append(REG_SOURCE_CHAIN)

    reports = []
    for repo_path, role in artifacts:
        reports.append(_report_for(
            repo_path, role, repo_root, claude_dir, home,
            settings_entries, git_entries, path_dirs,
            refs.get(repo_path.name, []), tuple(checked), tuple(skipped)))
    return reports


def _report_for(repo_path: Path, role: str, repo_root: Path, claude_dir: Path,
                home: str, settings_entries, git_entries, path_dirs,
                references, checked, skipped) -> HookReport:
    base = repo_path.name
    rel = os.path.relpath(str(repo_path), str(repo_root))

    if role == "bin":
        installed_path = Path(home) / ".local" / "bin" / base
    else:
        installed_path = claude_dir / rel

    wirings = []

    # (b) claude-settings — the registry everyone overlooked.
    for site, event, matcher, command in settings_entries:
        for target in referenced_paths(command, home):
            if os.path.basename(target) != base:
                continue
            wirings.append(Wiring(
                registry=REG_CLAUDE_SETTINGS, site=site,
                detail=f"{event}:{matcher or '*'}", command=command.strip(),
                target=target, target_exists=os.path.exists(target)))
            break

    # (a) git-hooks — installed AS a hook, or chained from one's body.
    for hook_file, real, body in git_entries:
        hook_name = os.path.basename(hook_file)
        if real == str(repo_path.resolve()) or os.path.basename(real) == base:
            wirings.append(Wiring(
                registry=REG_GIT_HOOKS, site=hook_file,
                detail=f"installed as git hook '{hook_name}'",
                target=real, target_exists=os.path.exists(real)))
            continue
        for i, line in enumerate(body.splitlines(), start=1):
            if base in line and _exec_shaped(line, base):
                targets = [t for t in referenced_paths(line, home)
                           if os.path.basename(t) == base]
                target = targets[0] if targets else ""
                wirings.append(Wiring(
                    registry=REG_GIT_HOOKS, site=f"{hook_file}:{i}",
                    detail=f"chained from git hook '{hook_name}'",
                    command=line.strip()[:200], target=target,
                    target_exists=bool(target) and os.path.exists(target)))
                break

    # (c) PATH — first match wins at exec time; later ones are shadowed.
    for idx, d in enumerate(path_dirs):
        cand = d / base
        if not cand.exists():
            continue
        detail = "PATH entry" if not any(
            w.registry == REG_PATH for w in wirings) else "PATH entry (shadowed)"
        try:
            real = str(cand.resolve())
        except OSError:
            real = str(cand)
        wirings.append(Wiring(
            registry=REG_PATH, site=str(d), detail=f"{detail} #{idx}",
            target=real, target_exists=os.path.exists(real)))

    # (d) source-chain — HEURISTIC. Test-only callers are excluded on purpose (a
    #     suite running a hook proves nothing about production), and so are
    #     non-executable files (prose cannot invoke anything).
    for r in references:
        if r.is_invocation():
            wirings.append(Wiring(
                registry=REG_SOURCE_CHAIN, site=f"{r.path}:{r.line}",
                detail="exec-shaped reference from a non-test repo file",
                command=r.text, target=str(repo_path),
                target_exists=repo_path.exists(), heuristic=True))

    exists = repo_path.exists()
    return HookReport(
        name=base, repo_path=rel, exists=exists, role=role,
        install=inspect_install(repo_path, installed_path),
        wirings=tuple(wirings), registries_checked=checked,
        registries_skipped=skipped, references=tuple(references),
        status=classify_wiring(exists, tuple(wirings), checked, skipped),
    )


# ============================================================================
# WIRING REPORT RENDERING
# ============================================================================

def wiring_to_dict(r: HookReport) -> dict:
    return {
        "name": r.name,
        "repo_path": r.repo_path,
        "role": r.role,
        "exists": r.exists,
        "status": r.status,
        "install": {
            "shape": r.install.shape,
            "path": r.install.path,
            "symlink_target": r.install.symlink_target,
            "tracks_repo": r.install.tracks_repo,
            "content": r.install.content,
        },
        "registries_wiring_it": list(r.registry_names()),
        "registries_checked": list(r.registries_checked),
        "registries_not_checked": [
            {"registry": reg, "reason": why} for reg, why in r.registries_skipped
        ],
        "wirings": [
            {"registry": w.registry, "site": w.site, "detail": w.detail,
             "command": w.command, "target": w.target,
             "target_exists": w.target_exists, "heuristic": w.heuristic}
            for w in r.wirings
        ],
        "invocation_sites": [
            {"path": f"{x.path}:{x.line}", "exec_shaped": x.exec_shaped,
             "test_only": x.is_test, "executable_file": x.executable_file,
             "counts_as_wiring": x.is_invocation(), "text": x.text}
            for x in r.references
        ],
    }


def render_wiring_report(reports) -> str:
    """Text report. Ordered loudest-first so the states that cost someone a
    conclusion are not buried under thirty healthy rows."""
    order = {W_WIRED_MISSING: 0, W_INDETERMINATE: 1, W_UNWIRED: 2,
             W_ABSENT: 3, W_ACTIVE: 4}
    rows = sorted(reports, key=lambda r: (order.get(r.status, 9), r.name))

    lines = ["# Hook registry-wiring matrix (SABLE-31b5l)", ""]
    lines.append("Registries enumerated (an artifact absent from one can be fully "
                 "live in another):")
    for reg in PRIMARY_REGISTRIES:
        lines.append(f"  * {reg}")
    for reg in HEURISTIC_REGISTRIES:
        lines.append(f"  * {reg}  [HEURISTIC — a miss here is not proof of absence]")
    lines.append("")
    if rows:
        lines.append("SCOPE: " + rows[0].scope_line())
        lines.append("")

    for r in rows:
        regs = ", ".join(r.registry_names()) or "-"
        lines.append(f"{r.status:<20} {r.name}  [{r.role}]")
        lines.append(f"    repo      {r.repo_path}")
        shape = r.install.shape
        if shape == SHAPE_SYMLINK:
            note = "tracks repo" if r.install.tracks_repo else "elsewhere"
            lines.append(f"    install   {shape} -> {r.install.symlink_target} ({note})")
        elif shape == SHAPE_COPY:
            lines.append(f"    install   {shape} {r.install.path} "
                         f"content={r.install.content} "
                         f"(does NOT track the repo; stale after the next landing)")
        else:
            lines.append(f"    install   {shape}")
        lines.append(f"    wired by  {regs}")
        for w in r.wirings:
            flag = " [HEURISTIC]" if w.heuristic else ""
            miss = "" if w.target_exists else "  *** TARGET MISSING ***"
            lines.append(f"      - {w.registry}: {w.detail} @ {w.site}{flag}{miss}")
        invocations = sum(1 for x in r.references if x.is_invocation())
        testonly = sum(1 for x in r.references if x.is_test)
        lines.append(f"    refs      {len(r.references)} textual "
                     f"({invocations} exec-shaped non-test, {testonly} test-only); "
                     f"a textual mention is not proof of invocation")
        if r.status in (W_UNWIRED, W_INDETERMINATE, W_WIRED_MISSING):
            lines.append(f"    scope     {r.scope_line()}")
        lines.append("")

    counts = {}
    for r in rows:
        counts[r.status] = counts.get(r.status, 0) + 1
    lines.append("## Summary")
    lines.append("")
    for st in ALL_WIRING_STATUSES:
        if counts.get(st):
            lines.append(f"  {st:<20} {counts[st]}")
    lines.append("")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def run_wiring(args) -> int:
    home = args.home or os.environ.get("HOME", "")
    repo_root = Path(args.repo) if args.repo else _repo_root()
    reports = build_wiring_matrix(
        repo_root=repo_root, home=home, path_env=args.path_env,
        settings_paths=args.settings, include_bin=args.include_bin,
        names=args.name or None,
    )
    if args.json:
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(
            {"repo": str(repo_root), "home": home,
             "registries": list(REGISTRIES),
             "heuristic_registries": list(HEURISTIC_REGISTRIES),
             "reports": [wiring_to_dict(r) for r in reports]},
            indent=2) + "\n")
    else:
        sys.stdout.write(render_wiring_report(reports))
    return 0


# ============================================================================
# CLI
# ============================================================================

def main(argv=None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="D6 repro-matrix harness + S4 classification doc")
    ap.add_argument("--emit-doc", metavar="PATH",
                    help="drive the matrix and write the classification doc to PATH")
    ap.add_argument("--print", action="store_true",
                    help="render the doc to stdout")
    ap.add_argument("--transcript", metavar="PATH",
                    help="count ground-truth tool_use from a real session transcript jsonl")
    ap.add_argument("--workdir", metavar="DIR",
                    help="scratch dir for real hook invocations (default: a tempdir)")

    # --- the registry-wiring matrix (SABLE-31b5l) ---
    ap.add_argument("--wiring", action="store_true",
                    help="report which REGISTRY wires each hook (git-hooks / "
                         "claude-settings / PATH / source-chain) plus install shape")
    ap.add_argument("--json", action="store_true",
                    help="with --wiring: emit the machine-readable report")
    ap.add_argument("--repo", metavar="DIR",
                    help="with --wiring: repo root to scan (default: this repo)")
    ap.add_argument("--home", metavar="DIR",
                    help="with --wiring: HOME to resolve ~/.claude and ~/.local/bin "
                         "against (use a SCRATCH home in tests; never the live one)")
    ap.add_argument("--path-env", metavar="PATH",
                    help="with --wiring: PATH value to check instead of $PATH")
    ap.add_argument("--settings", metavar="FILE", action="append",
                    help="with --wiring: settings.json to check (repeatable; "
                         "default: the user+project layering)")
    ap.add_argument("--include-bin", action="store_true",
                    help="with --wiring: also scan bin/sable-* (PATH-wired tools)")
    ap.add_argument("--name", metavar="BASENAME", action="append",
                    help="with --wiring: restrict to these artifact basenames")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    if args.wiring:
        return run_wiring(args)

    if not args.emit_doc and not args.print:
        ap.error("nothing to do: pass --emit-doc PATH, --print, and/or --wiring")

    if args.workdir:
        wd = Path(args.workdir)
        wd.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        results = build_matrix(wd, transcript=args.transcript)
    else:
        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix="sable-hook-matrix-") as tmp:
            results = build_matrix(Path(tmp), transcript=args.transcript)

    doc = render_doc(results)

    if args.emit_doc:
        out = Path(args.emit_doc)
        out.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        out.write_text(doc + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
        sys.stderr.write(f"wrote {out} ({len(results)} cells)\n")
    if args.print:
        sys.stdout.write(doc + "\n")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
